<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A missing sample under a PLACED clip must cost NOTHING but its sound.

  sample_missing_survives.qxa covers the easy half: there the cut over the
  missing wave is referenced by nothing. The hard half is this one — the clip on
  the absent sample is ON the track, at 96000, next to a good clip:

      track1 -> cut1 -> wave1 (../test_sawtooth.wav)   good, audible
             -> cut2 -> wave2 (nowhere on any machine) silent

  THE THREE BEHAVIOURS THIS FIXTURE HAS SEEN, in order:

    1. Before proposal 37 D8a it lost EVERYTHING. wave2 could not be
       instantiated, so cut2 could never resolve, so track1 could never resolve,
       so the mixer could not either, and the load ended on "root component of
       project was not found".
    2. D8a's prune-and-retry repaired by element KIND: an <SCut> is a WINDOW and
       a window whose content is gone has nothing to show, so cut2 was DROPPED;
       on the next pass track1 was a container with a dangling link, so the LINK
       went and the track stayed. The project loaded — one clip short, and the
       next save made that permanent.
    3. Now wave2 loads as a MISSING PLACEHOLDER, so cut2 never dangles and
       nothing is pruned at all. The clip keeps its position and its window and
       is SILENT; it plays again the moment the file is reachable.

  The prune-and-retry sweep is untouched and still guards every OTHER way a
  reference can dangle (an unknown element, a truncated file) — see
  load_unknown_object_survives.qxa. What changed is that a missing FILE is no
  longer one of those ways.

  The fixture ALSO declares formatVersion='99', i.e. it claims to come from a
  build newer than this one. That belongs here rather than in a case of its
  own: a reader must never refuse a document over its version (it would strand a
  user's file on whichever build they happen to have installed), so the correct
  behaviour is precisely the tolerance this case is about — warn, then load.

  WHAT BITES. The audio assertions catch a regression to step 1. Only
  `assert-extern-files` can catch a regression to step 2: the dropped clip and
  the placeholder are both SILENT, so no audio assertion anywhere can separate
  them — which is why step 2 shipped under a green suite in the first place.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="load_missing_sample_placed_survives">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <load-project path="../load_missing_sample_placed_survives.qxp"/>

    <!-- The recovery named the file it could not find. -->
    <assert-log contains="__missing_sample__.wav" minCount="1"/>

    <!-- A newer formatVersion warns and loads anyway. -->
    <assert-log contains="formatVersion '99'" minCount="1"/>

    <render filename="load_missing_sample_placed_survives.wav"
            format="wav" quality="10"/>

    <!-- 0-1 s: the surviving clip plays source sec1 (startOffset=48000) -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="load_missing_sample_placed_survives.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>

    <!-- 1-2 s: source sec2 -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="load_missing_sample_placed_survives.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.26" maxRms="0.33"/>
    <!-- The placeholder is IN the project, so cut2 is still on the track.
         Under the drop-and-prune behaviour this read count="1" missing="0".
         In <actions>, not <assertions>: that block dispatches two hardcoded
         kinds and knows no verbs. -->
    <assert-extern-files count="2" missing="1"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
